{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 38, "model_name": "abacusai/dracarys-llama-3.1-70b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187. He was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. Raymond III was born around 1140 and succeeded his father as Count of Tripoli in 1152.\n\n*Early Life and Reign*\nRaymond III was still a minor when he became Count of Tripoli. His mother Hodierna acted as regent until he came of age. In 1157 Raymond III married Hodierna of Milly. The marriage was likely arranged by his mother to strengthen the alliance between the County of Tripoli and the Kingdom of Jerusalem.\n\nIn 1164 Raymond III was captured by the forces of Nur ad-Din Zengi during the Battle of Inab. He was held captive for several years and was released in 1173. After his release Raymond III became a close ally of King Amalric I of Jerusalem. He participated in several military campaigns against the Muslims and was present at the Battle of Montgisard in 1177.\n\n*Conflict with the Templars*\nRaymond III had a complicated relationship with the Knights Templar. In 1173 he seized the Templar stronghold of Tortosa and held it for several years. The Templars eventually regained control of the stronghold in 1180. The conflict between Raymond III and the Templars continued until his death in 1187.\n\n*Later Life and Death*\nRaymond III died on September 29 1187 during the Battle of Hattin. He was succeeded by his godson Raymond IV Count of Tripoli. Raymond III's death marked the end of the direct line of the Counts of Tripoli. The County of Tripoli was eventually inherited by the descendants of Raymond IV.\n\n*Legacy*\nRaymond III played an important role in the history of the Crusader states. He was a key ally of the Kingdom of Jerusalem and participated in several important battles against the Muslims. Despite his conflicts with the Templars Raymond III remained a powerful and influential fi